WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.843
Embedded systems may store device
credentials, manufacturing data,

2
00:00:03.923 --> 00:00:07.945
keys, certificates, pairing material,
calibration data, configuration secrets,

3
00:00:08.025 --> 00:00:08.932
or service tokens.

4
00:00:09.012 --> 00:00:12.298
These values often affect identity,
access, updates, diagnostics,

5
00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:14.248
and communication with other systems.

6
00:00:14.328 --> 00:00:18.180
Rust lifetimes and ownership can help make
sensitive data flow easier to review.

7
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.810
A type can make clear where a secret
enters the system,

8
00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.269
which component owns it, where it may be
borrowed,

9
00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:25.049
and when it should no longer be used.

10
00:00:25.129 --> 00:00:28.075
But ownership alone does not erase
secrets, protect storage,

11
00:00:28.155 --> 00:00:30.252
or prevent disclosure through debug paths.

12
00:00:30.332 --> 00:00:33.278
Teams need clear policies for where
secrets are provisioned,

13
00:00:33.358 --> 00:00:36.757
where they are stored, whether
hardware-backed storage is available,

14
00:00:36.837 --> 00:00:39.783
who can access them, how credentials are
rotated or replaced,

15
00:00:39.863 --> 00:00:43.149
and what happens when a device is
serviced, transferred, or retired.

16
00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:46.912
These decisions belong in the firmware
lifecycle, not only in code comments.

17
00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:50.278
Debug output, logs, crash data,
diagnostics, manufacturing tools,

18
00:00:50.358 --> 00:00:52.624
and field-support scripts are exposure
paths.

19
00:00:52.704 --> 00:00:55.877
They should avoid printing keys, tokens,
private configuration,

20
00:00:55.957 --> 00:00:59.073
sensitive identifiers, or raw buffers that
may contain secrets.

21
00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:03.176
Redaction should be designed, not added
only after a release review finds a problem.

22
00:01:03.256 --> 00:01:06.939
Defensive embedded Rust avoids hard-coded
production secrets and plans for

23
00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:07.699
replacement.

24
00:01:07.779 --> 00:01:11.348
Device identity and credential handling
should be documented enough that

25
00:01:11.428 --> 00:01:15.507
engineering, manufacturing, support, and
security teams understand how sensitive

26
00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:18.420
material is introduced, protected,
updated, and removed.
